{"ratings":[{"id":"F1","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A symlinked worktree path can cause the stop check to miss a live process and allow overlapping gates to act on the same worktree."},{"id":"F2","consequence":"boundary","trigger":"plausible","reason":"If the daemon and surviving executor use different ports, gate code can reach the live daemon despite the intended confinement restriction."},{"id":"F3","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A live but stuck executor or failed cleanup can leave a run waiting indefinitely for the worktree."},{"id":"F4","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"A retry can lose setup prerequisites stored in scratch and report a real gate failure for a flaky check."},{"id":"F5","consequence":"wrong","trigger":"plausible","reason":"After an executor failure, fallback can grade files modified by the interrupted check instead of the committed contents."}]}